hes":"Anyone who ends up in a court case.","confidence":"title-only"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"A000055","score":0.05054435314671977},"overlapCount":80},"meaning":{"percentile":83.30967029499922,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.5712314830551443}},{"billId":"119-hr-2705","bioguideId":"M001235","cycle":2026,"score":0.018722190563126313,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":27300,"donorShare":0.02688796664804798,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.017110524230575984},{"industry":"hospitality","donorAmount":4500,"donorShare":0.004432082414513403,"billConfidence":0.4,"contribution":0.0016116663325503285}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.5712314830551443},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-2705","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"2705","title":"Nuclear Family Priority Act","introducedDate":"2025-04-08","latestActionDate":"2025-04-08","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Immigration","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001132","cosponsorCount":45,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/2705","industries":[{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Immigration law firms would see changes in demand for services related to family-based visa petitions and the new nonimmigrant parent visa category."},{"industry":"hospitality","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Reduced family immigration could affect demand for travel and lodging services related to family visits and relocation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would sharply reduce the number of family-based immigration visas available each year, lowering the annual cap from 480,000 to 88,000. It would eliminate visa categories for siblings and married children of U.S. citizens, and would remove parents of citizens from the immediate-relative category that currently has no numerical limit. Parents of citizens would instead be eligible for a temporary visa that does not allow them to work or receive public benefits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"K000405","score":0.03341706325741149},"overlapCount":41},"meaning":{"percentile":72.01451333017826,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.5712314830551443}},{"billId":"119-hjres-75","bioguideId":"M001235","cycle":2026,"score":0.01563742010366569,"matches":[{"industry":"retail-consumer","donorAmount":30000,"donorShare":0.02954721609675602,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.008234470059751678},{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":31500,"donorShare":0.031024576901593822,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.005085996213376037},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":4000,"donorShare":0.003939628812900803,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.0012270974991002502},{"industry":"hospitality","donorAmount":4500,"donorShare":0.004432082414513403,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.0010898563314377223}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.5712314830551443},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-75","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"75","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Office of Energy Efficiency and Renewable Energy, Department of Energy relating to \"Energy Conservation Program: Energy Conservation Standards for Commercial Refrigerators, Freezers, and Refrigerator-Freezers\".","introducedDate":"2025-03-10","latestActionDate":"2025-05-09","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-9.","policyArea":"Energy","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Department of Energy","Energy efficiency and conservation","Food industry and services","Lighting, heating, cooling"],"sponsorBioguideId":"G000601","cosponsorCount":13,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/75","industries":[{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"Manufacturers of commercial refrigeration equipment would no longer need to meet the stricter energy efficiency standards that were set to take effect in 2029."},{"industry":"retail-consumer","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Grocery stores, restaurants, and other food retailers that purchase and operate commercial refrigeration equipment would be affected by whether they must buy equipment meeting the new standards."},{"industry":"hospitality","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Hotels and restaurants use commercial refrigeration equipment and would be affected by the regulatory requirements for equipment they purchase."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Electric utilities would see different demand patterns depending on whether commercial refrigeration equipment must meet higher efficiency standards."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution would cancel a Department of Energy rule that set stricter energy efficiency standards for commercial refrigerators, freezers, and similar equipment used by businesses. The rule, published in January 2025, would have required this equipment to meet new energy conservation standards by January 2029. If this resolution passes, those updated standards would be eliminated and the equipment would not need to comply with the new requirements.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys food.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"N000189","score":0.0257116644139778},"overlapCount":14},"meaning":{"percentile":64.83672503549455,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.5712314830551443}},{"billId":"119-hr-29","bioguideId":"M001235","cycle":2026,"score":0.01559248803734525,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":27300,"donorShare":0.02688796664804798,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.013059869514766162},{"industry":"government","donorAmount":5000,"donorShare":0.0049245360161260035,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.002532618522579088}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.5712314830551443},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-29","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"29","title":"Laken Riley Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Read the second time. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 10.","policyArea":"Immigration","subjects":["Border security and unlawful immigration","Civil actions and liability","Crimes against property","Criminal investigation, prosecution, interrogation","Detention of persons","Immigration status and procedures","Intergovernmental relations","Refugees, asylum, displaced persons","State and local government operations","Visas and passports"],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001129","cosponsorCount":53,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/29","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill directly imposes new detention mandates on DHS and creates new litigation exposure for federal agencies, affecting federal and state government operations and budgets."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new grounds for state litigation against the federal government and expands detention requirements, increasing demand for legal services in immigration and intergovernmental litigation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would require the Department of Homeland Security to detain individuals who are in the country without authorization and have been arrested for or charged with burglary, theft, larceny, or shoplifting. It would also allow state governments to sue the federal government for injunctive relief when federal immigration enforcement decisions or failures—such as releasing individuals from custody or not fulfilling visa or asylum processing requirements—cause harm to the state or its residents, including financial harm exceeding $100.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here. People applying to come here, and their families here.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"S000250","score":0.02595346470272257},"overlapCount":50},"meaning":{"percentile":64.63164536993217,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.5712314830551443}},{"billId":"119-hr-27","bioguideId":"M001235","cycle":2026,"score":0.01112605516470951,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":27300,"donorShare":0.02688796664804798,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.01112605516470951}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.5712314830551443},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-27","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"27","title":"HALT Fentanyl Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Received in the Senate and Read twice and referred to the Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Crime and Law Enforcement","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Department of Justice","Drug trafficking and controlled substances","Licensing and registrations","Research administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"G000568","cosponsorCount":59,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/27","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill changes registration and licensing requirements for pharmaceutical researchers working with controlled substances and affects the regulatory classification of fentanyl-related compounds."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The bill establishes new criminal penalties and quantity thresholds that will affect defense attorneys and prosecutors handling drug trafficking cases."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would permanently classify all fentanyl-related substances as Schedule I controlled substances, meaning they would be treated as having high abuse potential and no accepted medical use. It sets the same criminal penalties and quantity thresholds for fentanyl-related substances as currently apply to fentanyl analogues. The bill also creates a streamlined registration process for researchers studying Schedule I substances and makes it easier for registered researchers to conduct certain activities without additional permits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People caught up in the criminal law, and victims of crime.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001086","score":0.08496351694573398},"overlapCount":58},"meaning":{"percentile":51.42766997949203,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.5712314830551443}},{"billId":"119-hjres-89","bioguideId":"M001235","cycle":2026,"score":0.01096686354067426,"matches":[{"industry":"construction","donorAmount":21000,"donorShare":0.020683051267729215,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.004596233615050936},{"industry":"agriculture","donorAmount":14500,"donorShare":0.01428115444676541,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.0027202198946219827},{"industry":"transport","donorAmount":8500,"donorShare":0.008371711227414206,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.002524801798743967},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":4000,"donorShare":0.003939628812900803,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.0011256082322573724}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.5712314830551443},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-89","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"89","title":"Providing congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"California State Motor Vehicle and Engine and Nonroad Engine Pollution Control Standards; The \"Omnibus\" Low NOX Regulation; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision\".","introducedDate":"2025-04-02","latestActionDate":"2025-06-12","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-17.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Air quality","California","Climate change and greenhouse gases","Congressional oversight","Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)","Federal preemption","Hybrid, electric, and advanced technology vehicles","Motor vehicles"],"sponsorBioguideId":"O000019","cosponsorCount":32,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/89","industries":[{"industry":"transport","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects emissions compliance obligations for manufacturers and operators of heavy-duty trucks, buses, and diesel-powered vehicles and equipment."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Vehicle and engine manufacturers would face different regulatory requirements depending on whether California's stricter emissions standards remain in effect or are preempted by federal rules."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Construction firms use off-road diesel equipment covered by the waiver, and the bill would affect compliance costs and equipment specifications for these machines."},{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Agricultural operations use off-road diesel engines and heavy-duty vehicles subject to the emissions standards that would be affected by revoking the waiver."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution would cancel a waiver that allows California to enforce stricter emissions standards than federal rules for heavy-duty trucks, buses, and off-road diesel equipment starting with 2024 models. The waiver, granted by the Environmental Protection Agency under the Clean Air Act, permits California to set its own limits on nitrogen oxide pollution from these vehicles and engines. If passed, the resolution would restore federal preemption and prevent California from enforcing these particular state-level standards.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who drives. People living near a road, a plant or a port.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"W000806","score":0.05252904442813575},"overlapCount":32},"meaning":{"percentile":50.8597570594731,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.5712314830551443}},{"billId":"119-hjres-87","bioguideId":"M001235","cycle":2026,"score":0.00900369334948371,"matches":[{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":31500,"donorShare":0.031024576901593822,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.005170762816932304},{"industry":"transport","donorAmount":8500,"donorShare":0.008371711227414206,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.002651041888681165},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":4000,"donorShare":0.003939628812900803,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.0011818886438702408}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.5712314830551443},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-87","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"87","title":"Providing congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"California State Motor Vehicle and Engine Pollution Control Standards; Heavy-Duty Vehicle and Engine Emission Warranty and Maintenance Provisions; Advanced Clean Trucks; Zero Emission Airport Shuttle; Zero-Emission Power Train Certification; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision\".","introducedDate":"2025-04-02","latestActionDate":"2025-06-12","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-15.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Air quality","California","Climate change and greenhouse gases","Federal preemption","Legislative rules and procedure","Motor carriers","Motor vehicles"],"sponsorBioguideId":"J000307","cosponsorCount":24,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/87","industries":[{"industry":"transport","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects regulatory obligations for trucking companies and heavy-duty vehicle operators subject to California emission standards."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill would change compliance requirements for manufacturers of heavy-duty trucks and engines sold in California."},{"industry":"energy-renewable","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Revoking zero-emission truck mandates would reduce market requirements for electric vehicle charging infrastructure and clean powertrain technology."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Changes to zero-emission vehicle requirements could affect electricity demand and infrastructure planning for fleet electrification."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would cancel waivers that allow California to enforce its own stricter emission standards for heavy-duty trucks and engines. Under the Clean Air Act, California can request permission to set its own vehicle pollution rules that are tougher than federal standards; this resolution would revoke several such waivers related to truck emissions, warranties, zero-emission airport shuttles, and advanced clean truck requirements. If passed, California would lose the authority to enforce these particular state-level vehicle pollution rules.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who drives. People living near a road, a plant or a port.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"L000566","score":0.030207281417461054},"overlapCount":24},"meaning":{"percentile":43.11405584477047,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.5712314830551443}},{"billId":"119-hres-47","bioguideId":"M001235","cycle":2026,"score":0.0006201267575862374,"matches":[{"industry":"education","donorAmount":1000,"donorShare":0.0009849072032252007,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.0006201267575862374}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.5712314830551443},"bill":{"id":"119-hres-47","congress":119,"billType":"hres","billNumber":"47","title":"Concerning the National Collegiate Athletic Association policy for eligibility in women's sports.","introducedDate":"2025-01-15","latestActionDate":"2025-01-15","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Education and Workforce.","policyArea":"Sports and Recreation","subjects":["Higher education","School athletics","Sex, gender, sexual orientation discrimination"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S001214","cosponsorCount":13,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-resolution/47","industries":[{"industry":"education","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The resolution directly addresses NCAA policy, which governs collegiate athletics at member universities and affects their compliance obligations and program administration."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Changes to NCAA eligibility rules could affect the composition and marketability of collegiate sports programming and events."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution calls on the National Collegiate Athletic Association to withdraw its current policy allowing transgender student-athletes to compete and instead adopt rules requiring participation in women's sports to be based on biological sex. The measure urges the NCAA to mandate that all member conferences implement such a policy across every sport and division. As a resolution, it expresses the position of the House but does not create enforceable law.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"College students, and their families.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001096","score":0.005818742346366307},"overlapCount":11},"meaning":{"percentile":2.003470578955671,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.5712314830551443}}],"votes":[{"id":"a64a90b759595d68f3a8d502","billId":"119-hr-7008","date":"2026-07-22T16:51:00-04:00","question":"Yea-and-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Yea","sourceUrl":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2026/roll280.xml"},{"id":"2af